= Outlook and decisions requested

== Plan for the third quarter

The third quarter has three substantive objectives. First, to run the rebooked
cross-calibration campaign between 8 and 19 September and close milestone M2.4.
Second, to complete the switched high concentration retrieval trial and reach a
decision on whether an operational product above 90 milligrams per litre is achievable
within this award. Third, to begin the observation operator development that replaces
the nearest neighbour lookup in the assimilation chain, using the effort released by
stopping the settling closure work.

Two secondary activities run alongside. The winter deployment hardware, purchased in
May, will be assembled and bench tested through August so that the laying campaign in
November is not the first time the revised clump weight configuration is handled. The
public access portal, milestone M4.3, enters user testing in September with the
Partnership as the test group.

An unplanned analysis has been added at low cost. The April campaign observed the
turbidity maximum approximately 1.4 kilometres upstream of its climatological position,
which the moored record shows persisted for eleven days. The programme will characterise
this excursion against the freshwater discharge record rather than let the observation
sit unused, because excursions of this magnitude bear directly on the tidal pumping term
in @eq:decomp and on the siting assumptions that underpin the array
@lindqvist2022turbidity.

== Forward risks

The dominant forward risk is seasonal compression. The September campaign, the November
laying and the December uncertainty budget merge fall in sequence with little slack, and
a failure in any one of them propagates. The programme has deliberately not attempted to
absorb this by compressing the schedule further; the mitigation is the contingency
platform for the campaign and the early hardware assembly for the laying, both of which
buy schedule rather than consume it.

The second forward risk is the winter storm exposure of the retrofitted moorings,
recorded as R2. The programme expects to learn more about mooring survivability in the
fourth quarter of 2026 than in the whole of the reporting period to date, and the
Oversight Board should expect the reliability picture in the next annual report to be
less favourable than the figures in this one, simply because the network will finally
have been tested.

== Decisions requested of the Oversight Board

The programme asks the Board to take four decisions. To note the slip of milestone M2.4
and approve the revised campaign dates of 8 to 19 September 2026. To endorse the
reallocation of WS2 effort from settling closure tuning to observation operator
development, on the evidence in @tab:modelskill. To note the positive expenditure
variance of 6.2 percent as a timing effect arising from the brought forward hardware
purchase, with the annual outturn forecast unchanged at 3 291.0 thousand euros. And to
approve the operational bound on the satellite retrieval, restricting its use to
concentrations below 90 milligrams per litre, for inclusion as a condition of use in the
guidance note issued to the Estuary Partnership.

No change to the approved workplan, the workstream structure or the annual allocation is
sought. The next quarterly report, covering July to September 2026, is due on 24 October
2026.
